## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of transactional memory in real workloads, careful fallback design, and low-latency queues.

## Key Concepts
- Intel TSX (_xbegin/_xend)
- CAS-based fallback paths
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Microbenchmarking for latency and throughput

## Code Example
```c
// Skeleton TSX-based enqueue
typedef struct { unsigned long long seq; unsigned long long val; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t *slots; unsigned head, tail, cap; } q_t;
static inline int enqueue_htm(q_t *q, unsigned long long val) {
    unsigned pos = q->tail;
    if ((pos + 1) % q->cap == q->head) return -1; // full

    unsigned status = _xbegin();
    if (status == _XBEGIN_STARTED) {
        q->slots[pos].val = val;
        q->slots[pos].seq = pos + 1;
        q->tail = (pos + 1) % q->cap;
        _xend();
        return 0;
    }

    // Fallback: CAS-based enqueue with sequence validation
    return enqueue_cas(q, val);
}
```

## Design Considerations
- Retry limits: 3-5 TSX attempts before permanent fallback
- Alignment: 64-byte cache line boundaries
- Contention handling: exponential backoff in fallback path
- Benchmark: measure 99th percentile latency under 1 vs 16 producer/consumer pairs

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of low-level synchronization primitives, atomic memory ordering semantics, and how barriers affect performance on modern cache-coherent CPUs.

## Key Concepts

- **Sense-reversal technique**: Allows barrier reuse by toggling a generation counter instead of creating new barrier objects
- **Atomic operations**: Use C11 `_Atomic` types with appropriate memory order primitives (`memory_order_acquire`, `memory_order_release`)
- **Spin-wait optimization**: Use CPU pause hints (`_mm_pause()`) to reduce memory contention and power consumption during busy-waiting
- **Memory ordering**: The arriving thread must use `memory_order_release` when updating the generation to ensure all prior writes are visible to waking threads; waiting threads use `memory_order_acquire` to synchronize with the releaser
- **Cache coherency**: On cache-coherent CPUs, the coherence protocol (e.g., MESI) ensures atomic updates propagate across cores, though false sharing can degrade performance—padding the atomic variables helps
- **Timeout handling**: Use `memory_order_relaxed` for the counter increment, then check timeout while spinning on generation; return an error code if timeout expires before the barrier releases
- **Fairness**: Threads are released simultaneously but may resume execution at slightly different times due to scheduling; no strict ordering is guaranteed

## Pseudo-Code Implementation

```c
typedef struct {
    _Atomic int count;
    _Atomic int generation;
    int N;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int N) {
    b->N = N;
    atomic_init(&b->count, 0);
    atomic_init(&b->generation, 0);
}

int barrier_wait(barrier_t *b) {
    int my_gen = atomic_load_explicit(&b->generation, memory_order_relaxed);
    if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) == b->N - 1) {
        atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, 0, memory_order_release);
        atomic_store_explicit(&b->generation, my_gen + 1, memory_order_release);
        return 1; // last thread
    }
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->generation, memory_order_acquire) == my_gen) {
        _mm_pause(); // avoid memory contention
    }
    return 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical design choices for low-latency allocators in multi-core, NUMA contexts, including cache locality, lock-free synchronization, and teardown safety.

## Key Concepts

- Per-core caches for locality
- Global lock-free freelist for cross-core refills
- Batch operations to amortize synchronization cost
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo: per-core cache node and refill logic
typedef struct Object { struct Object *next; } Object;
typedef struct { _Atomic(Object*) head; } FreeList;

#define BATCH 64

Object* allocate(FreeList *local, FreeList *global){
    Object* head = atomic_load_explicit(&local->head, memory_order_acquire);
    if(head){
        local->head = atomic_fetch_sub_explicit(&local->head, NULL, memory_order_acquire);
        return head;
    }
    // refill from global (simplified)
    for(int i=0;i<BATCH;i++){
        Object* o = pop_global(global);
        if(!o) break;
        push_local(local, o);
    }
    return allocate(local, global);
}

void deallocate(FreeList *local, Object *o){
    push_local(local, o);
}
```

